/-!
# Horizontal characters at an odd prime have even realizations

Theorem statement: `HorizontalPadicL.realizedHorizontalCharacter_even_of_odd_prime_v2`
(`81e913e3-4d03-46c8-bf4f-e2fed0c3cb4a`), by davidloeffler, 2026-09-25.

Proof: submission `ac31509f-85d4-4dd3-b3dc-267dc582c0e2`, by davidloeffler, 2026-09-25 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

The order of a realized horizontal character is a power of the odd prime p. Its value at -1 has
order at most two, so that value is one.

## Explanation of the source proof

The value of any character at $-1$ has order dividing two. The expected-properties hypothesis says
that the realized horizontal character has $p$-power order, so the order of its value at $-1$ also
divides a power of $p$. When $p$ is odd, the only order dividing both two and a power of $p$ is one.
Thus the value at $-1$ is one.

